[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1119,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 1158,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 157327,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 238,
        "headingId": 1158,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Leiomyosarcoma (LMS) of bone is a primary malignant neoplasm of bone showing smooth muscle differentiation.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:46:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1120,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 1159,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 157328,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 238,
        "headingId": 1159,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8890/3 Leiomyosarcoma NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:46:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1121,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 1160,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 157329,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 238,
        "headingId": 1160,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2B58 &amp; XH7ED4 Leiomyosarcoma &amp; Leiomyosarcoma NOS</p>\r\n<p>2B58 &amp; XA5GG8 Leiomyosarcoma &amp; Bones</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:46:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1122,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 1161,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 157330,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 238,
        "headingId": 1161,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:46:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1123,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 1162,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 157331,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 238,
        "headingId": 1162,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:46:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1124,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 1163,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 157332,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 238,
        "headingId": 1163,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most lesions (70%) occur in the long bones of the lower extremity around the knee (distal femoral or proximal tibial metaphysis), followed by the upper extremities (25%) and (more rarely) by the craniofacial skeleton {9351566; 11956991; 21393038; 40626672}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:46:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1125,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 1164,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 157333,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 238,
        "headingId": 1164,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients typically report pain and occasionally present with pathological fracture. Metastatic disease from extraosseous primary lesions (uterus, bowel, soft tissue) should be excluded.</p>\r\n<h5>Imaging features</h5>\r\n<p>Radiographically, they are lytic, aggressive lesions, with a permeative growth pattern and cortical destruction. MRI shows soft tissue extension in most cases, and signal intensities are nonspecific {11685485; 18603190}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:46:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1126,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 1165,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 157334,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 238,
        "headingId": 1165,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>It is very rare for LMS to occur primarily in bones, with an estimated incidence of 0.7% of all bone malignancies. There is a wide age distribution in a case series, reporting a mean age of 26.5&nbsp;years (range: 12&ndash;69&nbsp;years), with a peak in cases within the fourth and fifth decade of life and a clear male predominance [[Rohela H, Sharma A, Sahu V, et al. A decade of insight: Understanding primary leiomyosarcoma of the bone. Journal of Orthopaedic Reports. 2025;4(1):100556. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jorep.2025.100556.]].</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:46:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1127,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 1166,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 157335,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 238,
        "headingId": 1166,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A small subset of LMSs of bone are associated with prior exposure to radiation therapy or with bone infarcts {9872562; 18521345}. LMS of bone related to EBV infection in immunocompromised patients has been reported {16330945}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:46:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1128,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 1167,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 157336,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 238,
        "headingId": 1167,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Similar to deep soft tissue LMSs, intraosseous lesions show genomic losses and absence of phosphorylated RB1 {8644845; 15668899}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:46:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1135,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 1174,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 157337,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 238,
        "headingId": 1174,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The lesions vary widely in size. On cut surface, they show a tan, fleshy appearance, with obvious areas of necrosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:46:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1129,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 1168,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 157338,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 238,
        "headingId": 1168,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The lesions resemble LMSs from other locations, with cells arranged in long, intersecting fascicles, growing in an infiltrative pattern. The tumour cells have fibrillary, eosinophilic cytoplasm and elongated, cigar-shaped nuclei with blunted ends. The tumours are associated with variable degrees of necrosis, nuclear pleomorphism, and mitotic activity. Smooth muscle differentiation is demonstrated by diffuse staining with desmin and/or h-caldesmon, as well as SMA {21393038}. LMP1 immunohistochemistry or in&nbsp;situ hybridization for EBV RNA can be used to document EBV protein or RNA {16330945; 29605279}. Positivity for ER or PR in female patients strongly suggests a primary uterine origin.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:46:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1130,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 1169,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 157339,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 238,
        "headingId": 1169,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:46:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1131,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 1170,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 157340,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 238,
        "headingId": 1170,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:46:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1134,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 1173,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 157341,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 238,
        "headingId": 1173,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> bone tumour with compatible imaging; exclusion of other primary sites; intersecting fascicles of fusiform cells with eosinophilic fibrillary cytoplasm and elongated cigar-shaped nuclei, with variable nuclear pleomorphism; SMA and desmin and/or h-caldesmon immunoreactivity.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:46:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1132,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 1171,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 157342,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 238,
        "headingId": 1171,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Staging is according to bone sarcoma protocols (see &lt;&gt;<em>TNM staging of tumours of bone</em>). See also the information on staging in <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/87/261\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Bone tumours: Introduction</em>.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:46:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1133,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 1172,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 157343,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 238,
        "headingId": 1172,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Histological grade correlates with clinical outcome {9351566}. High-grade lesions are highly aggressive, with a high index of distant spread (often to the lung) and a 5-year disease-free survival rate of &lt;&nbsp;50% {19722754; 27302734; 40626672}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:46:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]